<% content_for :title, "Tools — Tinkerex" %>

<div class="page-head"><h1>Tools</h1></div>

<%# A CARD'S OWN MENU IS ITS RIGHT CLICK (`toolmenu`: toolmenu_controller.js, ShellHelper#tool_menu_data),
    and it holds what a card no longer draws (2026-11-23, the owner's call): Pin to sidebar, on every
    runnable card, the calculator and each addon a manifest made alike; and, on an addon's card,
    Permissions…, which opens the dialog rendered for it at the foot of this page. The card's visible
    Pin toggle (UX audit G2, 2026-11-02) and its "This tool can:" footer are GONE, so a card is its
    mark, its name and its sentence. Two costs, both accepted: iOS fires no contextmenu, so there a
    tool is pinned from nowhere (a phone has no sidebar to pin to anyway) and its permissions are
    read on its own page (tools/addons/show lists them, with the author's reasons); and the menu is
    invisible until tried, which is why Help says it in words. A greyed-out card has no menu: it
    cannot run here, so a pinned link to it could open nothing. `tools-home` scopes this page's card
    rules away from the bundles, which reuse `.tools-grid` and `.addon-card` for their own galleries
    (pages/tools.css). %>
<div class="tools-home" data-controller="toolmenu">

<%# WHY A TOOL NEVER APPEARED, for the author editing a bundle — development and test only
    (Tools::HomeController#index decides). Production makers never see it; the log keeps the clue.
    Above the sections, where the author looking for a missing card reads first. %>
<% if @addon_problems.any? %>
  <div class="form-errors flash-alert addon-problems" role="status">
    <%# "PROBLEMS", NOT "TOOLS THAT WEREN'T LOADED": a duplicate key is reported while both entries
        are still served (Registry.problems), so the count is of problems, not of missing tools. %>
    <p><%= pluralize(@addon_problems.size, "problem") %> in <code>manifests.json</code>:</p>
    <ul>
      <% @addon_problems.each do |problem| %>
        <li><code><%= problem[:key] %></code> — <%= problem[:reason] %></li>
      <% end %>
    </ul>
  </div>
<% end %>

<%# IN SECTIONS BY WHAT A TOOL DOES, built-in and add-on alike (Tools::HomeController::SECTIONS
    names the four and their order, BUILT_IN places the two built-in tools, and a manifest's
    `category` places an addon). Computed in the controller, an empty one never drawn. There is no
    "Built in" or "Add-ons" heading (2026-11-23, the owner's call): where a tool comes from is not
    what a maker opens this page to choose by. A built-in card stays `.tool-card` and every addon
    card `.addon-card`, so the count home_controller_test.rb pins keeps meaning "cards a manifest
    made". `index` is the card's position in @addons, not in its section, so title and dialog ids
    stay unique across sections. %>
<% @sections.each do |heading, description, built_ins, addons| %>
<% section_id = "tools-section-#{heading.parameterize}" %>
<section class="tools-section" aria-labelledby="<%= section_id %>">
<h2 class="tools-section-head" id="<%= section_id %>"><%= heading %></h2>
<p class="tools-section-desc muted"><%= description %></p>
<div class="tools-grid">
  <% built_ins.each do |key| %>
    <%= render "tools/home/#{key}_card" %>
  <% end %>
  <% addons.each do |addon| %>
    <% index = @addons.index(addon) %>
    <% if addon.available? %>
      <%#
        THE CARD IS A <div> AND ITS TITLE IS THE LINK (2026-11-02), stretched over the whole card by
        `.addon-card-link::after` (pages/tools.css): the card opens the tool from anywhere on it, and
        the link's name is the tool's name instead of every word on the card. It became a <div> to
        hold the pin <form>, which has gone (2026-11-23); it stays one because a link named by the
        title alone is the better link. The right-click data is on the card, so a right click
        anywhere on it opens the menu, and the menu hands focus back to the title link.
        `tool-permissions` names this card's dialog below, which is what puts Permissions… in it.
      %>
      <% title_id = "tool-title-#{index}" %>
      <%= tag.div class: "addon-card",
                  data: tool_menu_data(addon.key, addon.title, tool_permissions: "tool-permissions-#{index}") do %>
        <span class="addon-card-head">
          <%= render "tools/home/addon_mark", addon: %>
          <%= link_to addon.title, tools_addon_path(addon.key), class: "addon-title addon-card-link", id: title_id %>
        </span>
        <span class="addon-desc muted"><%= addon.description %></span>
      <% end %>
    <% else %>
      <%# NOT A LINK (UX audit G3): it cannot run here, and its page says the same sentence
          (tools/addons/show) rather than mounting a bundle that would fail. %>
      <div class="addon-card disabled" title="<%= addon.unavailable_reason %>">
        <span class="addon-card-head">
          <%= render "tools/home/addon_mark", addon: %>
          <span class="addon-title"><%= addon.title %></span>
        </span>
        <span class="addon-desc muted"><%= addon.unavailable_reason %></span>
      </div>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>
  <%# NO HARDCODED CARDS for an addon. The last one was openscad's, kept while it still had its own
      controller; it migrated to a bundle and the block went with it. Every `.addon-card` above
      comes from a manifest, which is what makes publishing a bundle the whole deploy. An addon
      card that has to be written here is the signal that an addon has not actually migrated. %>
</div>
</section>
<% end %>

<%# THE FIRST-RUN STATE, BELOW THE BUILT-IN TOOLS. With no addons this page was a title and a
    thousand pixels of void — the worst first-run surface in the app (UI-cohesion spec §2.4), and
    reached by every fresh self-host, since a box with no addon source configured lands here with
    nothing. The built-in tools now stand above it in their sections, so it says "add-ons", never
    "tools": two tools are on the page. It says what an add-on IS before it says there are none,
    and it distinguishes "none configured" from "configured but nothing came back", because those
    need different things done about them and the only existing signal was a `Rails.logger.warn`
    nobody reads.

    TWO READERS, SO TWO LAYERS (onboarding, 2026-10-13). The maker reads the paragraph and can do
    nothing about either state but ask, so it says what is true in their words and points at Help.
    The settings that fix it belong to the person running the server and sit behind the collapsed
    line, where help_page_test.rb asserts they still are: moved, not deleted. %>
<% if @addons.empty? %>
  <div class="empty">
    <p>Add-ons are small apps that run inside Tinkerex and save their output straight into
      your library. The box generator and the OpenSCAD editor are two.</p>
    <% if @addon_source_configured %>
      <p class="muted">No add-ons came back from this server just now. The person running it can
        check on them — <%= link_to "Help", help_path(anchor: "tools") %> says how.</p>
      <details class="muted">
        <summary>For the person running this server</summary>
        An addon source is configured, but it returned nothing. It may be unreachable, or it may
        be serving no bundles yet.
      </details>
    <% else %>
      <p class="muted">No add-ons are installed on this server. The person running it can add
        them — <%= link_to "Help", help_path(anchor: "tools") %> says how.</p>
      <details class="muted">
        <summary>For the person running this server</summary>
        Point <code>ADDON_SOURCE_URL</code> at a bundle server, or <code>ADDON_SOURCE_PATH</code>
        at a directory of bundles, and they appear here.
      </details>
    <% end %>
  </div>
<% end %>

<%# ONE PERMISSIONS DIALOG PER RUNNABLE ADDON, opened from its card's right-click menu. Out here,
    not inside the card: a closed <dialog> costs nothing anywhere, but one inside the card would sit
    under the card's contextmenu action and stretched link, so a right click or a click in the open
    dialog would reach the card. Server-rendered, so the labels come from the one vocabulary
    (Maker::Addons::Permissions) and no manifest string is ever assembled into markup in JS. %>
<% @addons.each_with_index do |addon, index| %>
  <% next unless addon.available? %>
  <%= render "tools/home/permissions_dialog", addon:, dialog_id: "tool-permissions-#{index}" %>
<% end %>
</div>
